          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear as a present emotional and relational state. The immediate qualification limits its extent but does not weaken the clear truth-presenting form of the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and presents her love as rendered in accordance with that obligation. She does not make a new promise or undertake unlimited future devotion; rather, she affirms the standing duties owed by daughter to father.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal words are a controlled, propositional avowal, explicitly measured by Cordelia's bond. Her difficulty verbalizing her heart supplies emotional context, but the expression is not a reflex-like discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured by avowal and invocation of filial obligation. Its role as an answer in Lear's love test does not establish a further independent force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks Cordelia to say how much she loves him in order to obtain a richer share. After initially answering “Nothing,” she gives this restrained avowal, defining her love by her filial bond rather than competing in expansive rhetoric.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes, rather than newly creates, her standing daughterly obligations to love, obey, and honour Lear. No distinct future promise is made. Lear rejects the bounded declaration as inadequate, urges her to amend it, and then disinherits and repudiates her; thus her account of the bond receives no accepting uptake from him. The supplied later passages nevertheless depict her concern and action for him as evidence of affection, not as a separate promise transferred onto the focal words.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is William Shakespeare's King Lear, specifically the opening love-test scene.\",\"limitations\":\"The classification is confined to the marked occurrence spoken by Cordelia to Lear. Immediate continuation clarifies its force, while later supplied passages inform evidence of affection; neighbouring declarations and later actions are not treated as part of the focal expression itself.\"}"
        }
